I have always been fascinated with  Vietnam and felt that it was a seminal  event in our nation's history and you  know welcome the idea to do a film about  the last days of Vietnam but I felt that  Vietnam has been so covered by  documentaries and books and I didn't  know if I could add anything new to the  equation and you know what I think a lot  of us are familiar with the iconic image  of the helicopter coming off of what we  think is the embassy it's not in fact  the Embassy as we all now know that was  really what I know right and there was  so much I didn't know and once I  uncovered the events and how dramatic  they were and how extraordinary they  were I was kind of blown away about how  much I didn't know and then when you  know we started uncovering these  extraordinary stories of Stuart  Harrington and the USS Kirk and people  Americans and Vietnamese who went  against US policy to sacrifice  themselves and to save the lives of the  Vietnamese and I was really hooked and I  think I also was excited about this film  because of the timeliness at that point  we were on the brink of getting out of  Iraq and Afghanistan and I felt even  though these were events that took place  40 years ago they have a lot of  resonance today you know there are  images coming out of Iraq where families  are desperately trying to get on  American helicopters and it feels like  Saigon 40 years ago the story itself is  just in its DNA is this amazingly  dramatic story it has this structure  that is it's almost you know like a  disaster film where the tsunamis coming  or them you know the bad guys were  coming and there's a ticking clock and  everybody has to try and get out and and  so you know we had that to start with  and we wrote up a treatment and it was a  very brief document because it came  across really quickly how you know  engaging and compelling and terrifying a  story it would be well I must say I was  quite astounded after we opened this  marvelous film that this marvelous lady  made at the Sundance Film Festival and  the audience was sizable audience young  and old and they came to me and they  said almost to the voice we never know  we never knew and it dawned on me that  half the people in the audience weren't  born at the time and the other half had  focused on Watergate the yom kippur war  the quadrupling of a barrel of crude and  the price and the impact on the economy  and that even though this was something  that I lived and breathed for the better  part of four years of my life it seemed  so much different to most people and one  of the things that I really will always  be grateful to Rory is that she did a  film about those events which instead of  being the was me weeping and gnashing of  teeth of a horrible we are as a country  focused on that side that dimension of  the story that normally doesn't get any  attention we really tried to contain  this story to this moment in time and as  much as we could take the politics out  of it and you know as Mark said there's  no experts there's no narrator there's  nobody looking back giving opinions  about this moment it's all the  first-hand accounts of what happened you  know there's nothing that you know that  the film tells you that the people in  the film don't know so you're learning  it as they learn it and so you know our  hope was that the experience would be as  though you were there one of my  experiences after Vietnam and other wars  that I had the misfortune or fortune to  be in was that I would always finally  get out from wherever it was whether it  was Desert Storm or the invasion of  Panama or Vietnam and then I'd see the  coverage of it in the documentaries and  I'd always ask myself you know if that  was Vietnam where was I that said in  this particular one it brings it home to  me with such a vivid real  ilysm that even 40 years later it  catches me emotionally by leaving the  commentary in the description of events  up to the people who were there you get  something that you rarely find these  days and that is its historicity its  veracity its absolute verra similis  verisimilitude do you have any hopes do  you think there may be any way that this  could help to change people's  perceptions about if not the overall  legacy then some of the human impact and  the human performances of the time I  think so although Vietnam has always  been such a contentious issue and I  certainly agree with Rory and mark that  to skirt around the politics of it was  the only way to a the only way to go  here there are still die hard camps of  people out there who made up their minds  about Vietnam 20 30 40 years ago and  they'll probably never change their  minds I think in line with what you're  saying politically you know I it sort of  it when we finish this film I looked at  it and I thought gosh Nixon comes across  pretty well in this film and Kissinger's  pretty good and maybe we shouldn't have  gotten out of Vietnam and you know kind  of all these ideas that are very counter  to my kind of lefty perspective on  things in the end when our policy  collapses and out on the ground are all  these people who because policy  decisions were made were deployed out  there and you can put in here Vietnam or  you can put Iraq or Afghanistan people  are out there to implement these  policies and if the policy is ill  thought out if as in the case of Vietnam  you grossly underestimate the North  Vietnamese and overestimate the South  Vietnamese what you get is what you saw  on the screen here and these human  events can really claw at your heart so  to know and to experience war firsthand  or the consequences of a failed war is  to as a person get this sense that these  policy decisions beget these human  events  and this is how it translates on the  ground one of the lessons that I think  I've learned in making this film is that  by april of nineteen seventy five there  were very few good options available and  you know had the head congress passed  722 million i think it would have made a  difference i don't think it would have  changed the tide of what happened so i  think the lesson to me is that once you  start a war you can lose control over it  and then you can get into a situation  like this quite easily and so when we're  thinking about these engagements with  Isis and going back into Iraq and Syria  I think it's really important imperative  that our leaders answer the questions  that what is our exit strategy what is  our plan what is our responsibility to  the people on the ground thank
 

